+# How to run the load test (US-30)
+
+Requirement MLE-NF-003: with 20 users at the same time, the average and the 95th-percentile response time are 3 seconds or less, with no failed requests.
+
+```
+pip install locust
+python member2_dev/manage.py migrate
+python loadtest/seed.py                                  # 20 users + a small dataset each
+python member2_dev/manage.py runserver 127.0.0.1:8000 --noreload      # in one terminal
+locust -f loadtest/locustfile.py --headless -u 20 -r 5 -t 90s --host http://127.0.0.1:8000 --csv docs/member2/reports/load_test
+```
+
+The last line printed says `LOAD TEST PASSED` or `FAILED`, and the exit code is 0 or 1, so it can run in GitHub Actions.
+
+Each simulated user logs in, fetches a CSRF token like a browser, then repeats every 2-4 seconds: list models (weight 5), check a job (4), validate parameters (3), start a random-forest training job (2). A "429 too many jobs" reply to a second Train click is the designed behaviour and counts as success; any other unexpected reply is a failure.
+
+The same program with other numbers gives a heavier test, for example `-u 50`. Login is the slowest request (about 1.3 s) because password hashing is deliberately slow; it happens once per user.
