+# US-30: Load test
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Epic | Quality / performance |
+| Requirement | MLE-NF-003 |
+| Points / sprint | 5 / Sprint 2 |
+| Owner | Varnika (Member 2) |
+
+**Story.** As a user in a lab session I want the site to stay responsive when about twenty people use it together.
+
+## Pass criteria and result (20 users, 90 seconds)
+
+| Measure | Required | Measured |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Average response time | <= 3000 ms | **64 ms** |
+| 95th percentile | <= 3000 ms | **130 ms** |
+| Failed requests | 0 | **0** of 596 |
+
+Slowest request type: login, 1.3 s on average (password hashing, once per user). Training requests answered in 45 ms on average because the job runs in the background; **74 real training jobs finished** during the test with none failed, and the server log had no errors.
+
+Raw data: `docs/member2/reports/load_test_stats.csv` (and the other `load_test_*.csv`). How to repeat it: `docs/member2/LOAD_TEST.md`.
+
+## Files
+
+`loadtest/locustfile.py` (user behaviour and automatic pass check), `loadtest/seed.py` (users and data), `member2_dev/views.py` (dev-only CSRF cookie endpoint).
+
+## Honest notes (for the viva)
+
+- First attempt: 47% of requests failed with 403. This was Django's CSRF protection rejecting simulated users that did not send the token, as designed; the test now behaves like a browser. The protection stays on.
+- Measured against Django's development server, SQLite and a 2-CPU container on one machine, with the load generator on the same machine. It shows the design is sound at this size; the final figure on the deployment machine may differ, so re-run it there.
+- The per-user rate limit (60 per minute) is not hit: simulated users make about 20 requests a minute.
